# SUIT Technical Guidelines 2025
# Based on the official SUIT guidelines document

## General Requirements

- All code, comments, and documentation must be in **English**
- Use Rider as the IDE (JetBrains Rider)
- Follow the style guide (standard in Rider)
- All submissions via Git following Git style guide
- No Git GUIs except Rider
- C# code must be adequately commented (methods and classes using XML comments)
- Sources of external 3D models, sounds, or other files must be documented
- Unity conventions must be followed
- Code should be meaningfully commented (not everything needs a comment, but some things are incomprehensible without one)

## C# Code Style Guide

### Naming Conventions
- **Methods**: Start with uppercase letter (PascalCase)
  - Example: `CalculateDistance()`, `MovePlayer()`
- **Private attributes/fields**: Start with underscore
  - Example: `_playerPosition`, `_isActive`
- **Variables**: Use descriptive names without abbreviations when possible
  - Good: `xPosAtTop`, `xTop`, `index`
  - Bad: `xPAT`, `xT`, `i` (unless in a simple loop)
- **Almost everything**: Use CamelCase
- **Namespaces**: Must correspond to folder structure

### Code Quality
- Avoid syntactic redundancy:
  - ❌ `if (boolVar == true) {}` → ✅ `if (boolVar) {}`
  - Prefer `foreach` loops when they work just as well as `for` loops
- Use standard C# conventions (enforced in Rider)
- Rider will underline deviations - right-click and fix them
- Format code properly:
  - Indentations and spaces matter!
  - Spaces before and after `=`, `!=`, `==`, etc.
  - Use Rider's auto-format: Select code → Hamburger Menu → Code Reformat Code (or Ctrl+Alt+L)

### Comments
- **Classes and Methods**: Use XML comments with `///`
  - In Rider: Type `///` before a method/class to auto-generate XML comment structure
  - AI can help generate these comments
- **Everything else**: Use regular `//` comments before or after the code
- Comments should be meaningful - not everything needs a comment, but complex logic does

### Example XML Comment Structure:
```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Moves the player to the specified position.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="position">The target position in world space.</param>
/// <returns>True if movement was successful, false otherwise.</returns>
public bool MovePlayer(Vector3 position)
{
    // Implementation
}
```

## Git Guidelines

### Branch Naming
- Format: `<category>/<surname>/<YYYYMMDD>_<description-with-dashes>`
- Categories: `level`, `interaction`, `bugfix`, `feature`, `pillnitz`
- Only lowercase letters
- Elements separated by underscores
- Example: `level/kulikov/20240131_implement-level-1`
- For group work: Alphabetically sorted surnames with single dash: `level/kulikov-wacker/20240131_implement-level-1`

### Commits
- **Language**: English
- **Frequency**: Push as early as possible
- **Atomic commits**: Not too large, not too small
  - No monster commits, but also not every line a separate commit
  - Try to separate by function:
    - "Added cat"
    - "Implemented swarm behavior script"
    - "Created scene with cats using swarm behavior"

### Commit Messages
- Follow a simplified version of Conventional Commits
- Three commit types (message must start with these):
  - `feat: added new cool functionality`
  - `fix: fixed some bug`
  - `chore: removed redundant files or updated something that had no visible effect`
- Follow with a very short summary
- For larger commits, add details after a blank line:
  ```
  feat: add some cool things for topic A
  
  * implement some stuff
  * improve other stuff
  ```

### Git Workflow
- Before each commit: Update local branch state
  - IDE: Hamburger Menu → Git → Fetch
  - Branch selection → Click current branch → Update
- New files are untracked (red in IDE) - must be added first
  - Right-click file → Git → Add
- File colors in IDE:
  - Red → untracked
  - Green → tracked and new
  - Blue → tracked and edited
  - Gray → tracked and will be removed

## Unity Guidelines

### Naming Conventions
- **All naming**: Use CamelCase
- Imported assets don't need to be renamed
- Pay attention to plural and singular in folders and files

### Folder Structure

#### Standard Structure (for smaller projects):
```
Assets/
  Scenes/
    Main.unity
  Prefabs/
    Dog.prefab
    Cat.prefab
  Materials/
    CatMaterial.mat
    DogMaterial.mat
  Scripts/
    PetMove.cs
  Textures/
    CatRed.jpg
    CatBlack.jpg
    Dog.jpg
  Imported_cat_From_website/
    cat_model.obj
  Dog_V2_from_WebSite/
    husky.fbx
```

#### Concepts Structure (for larger projects):
```
Assets/
  Scenes/
    Main.unity
  Concepts/
    Pets/
      Cat/
        Cat.prefab
        Textures/
          CatRed.jpg
          CatBlack.jpg
      Dog/
        Dog.prefab
        Dog.jpg
        Move.cs
    Deps/  (can also be at respective object)
      Imported_cat_From_website/
        cat_model.obj
      Dog_V2_from_WebSite/
        husky.fbx
```

### Unity Project Setup
- Work in `SampleScene`, not `SampleARFScene`
- Define project name early in your branch (include surnames)
- Example: "Call of Escape Room (Gottschild, Kulikov, Wacker)"
- Build APK and deploy to Quest after checkout

## AI Usage Guidelines

- **AI usage is allowed** (like copying from StackOverflow)
- **Must understand** copied/generated code and learn from it
- For larger or complex copied passages, add source as comment link
- Available tools:
  - ChatGPT
  - GitHub Copilot/Codex
  - JetBrains AI Assistant (Claude Agent, 5 free credits, but token-hungry)
  - LM-Studio for local AIs

## Project-Specific Notes

- Documentation is at the code itself
- README files exist in various folders
- If something is unclear, report it immediately - will be added to documentation
- For VR projects: Work in SampleScene, build APK and deploy to Quest

## Code Review Checklist

When reviewing or writing code, ensure:
- [ ] All methods and classes have XML comments (`///`)
- [ ] Variable names are descriptive (no unnecessary abbreviations)
- [ ] Code follows C# conventions (no Rider warnings)
- [ ] No syntactic redundancy (`if (bool == true)` → `if (bool)`)
- [ ] Proper formatting (spaces, indentation)
- [ ] Comments are in English
- [ ] External asset sources are documented
- [ ] Unity naming conventions followed (CamelCase)
- [ ] Folder structure follows Unity guidelines

